## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to reason about memory cgroup enforcement, scalable reclaim under mixed workloads, and kernel-level resource management.

## Key Concepts
- Per-container quotas and deficit calculation
- Per-page state tracking (active, inactive, referenced)
- NUMA-aware page replacement decisions
- Interaction with swap and lazy reclaim mechanisms
- Concurrency-safe quota accounting
- Starvation prevention and thrashing avoidance

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal C-like skeleton of reclaim loop
for (each node in nodes) {
    while (deficit[node] > 0) {
        // Select overcommitted cgroup as victim
        struct cgroup *victim = pick_victim_cgroup(node);

        // Evict pages from victim (prefer inactive, anon > file)
        struct page *page = lru_scan(victim, node);
        if (page) {
            if (try_to_reclaim(page))
                deficit[node] -= PAGE_SIZE;
        }
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical skills for implementing sleep/wake in a preemptive kernel, focusing on data structures, ISR interaction, and race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Sleep list ordered by wake_tick
- Per-task wake_tick and state fields
- Interrupt masking or locks to avoid races between enqueue and timer
- Immediate wakeup and re-scheduling to ready queue

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo C-like snippet
typedef struct Task { int wake_tick; int state; struct Task* next; } Task;
Task* sleep_list = NULL;
void sleep(int n) {
  int w = current_tick + n;
  task->wake_tick = w;
  disable_interrupts();
  insert_ordered(&sleep_list, task, w);
  task->state = SLEEPING;
  schedule();
  enable_interrupts();
}
void timer_handler() {
  current_tick++;
  while (sleep_list && sleep_list->wake_tick <= current_tick) {
    Task* t = pop_head(&sleep_list);
    t->state = READY;
    enqueue_ready(t);
  }
}
```

